<section class="[ dark:bg-stone-900 ] [ flex flex-col gap-5 ] [ p-5 ]">
    <Breadcrumbs CurrentTitle="C# and .NET Interview Preparation" CurrentUrl="@PageUrl" />
    <header class="[ flex flex-col gap-4 ]">
        <h1 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-bold text-3xl ]">C# and .NET Interview Questions and Answers</h1>
        <p>
            Prepare for a .NET developer interview with C# questions and answers, ASP.NET Core fundamentals,
            and system design interview practice. Start with language and runtime concepts, then explain how you
            design, test, deploy, and operate a real application. Use the answers as a starting point, not a script to memorize.
        </p>
        <p>
            New to .NET? Start with the C# fundamentals below. Preparing for an experienced or senior developer role?
            Work through the system design exercises and the production-readiness checklist, using examples from your own work.
            This free guide covers modern .NET; .NET Core is the older name used for releases before .NET 5.
        </p>
    </header>

    <nav aria-label="Interview preparation topics">
        <ul class="[ list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 ]">
            <li><a class="underline" href="interview/#csharp-interview-questions">C# interview questions and answers</a></li>
            <li><a class="underline" href="interview/#dotnet-interview-questions">.NET and ASP.NET Core interview questions</a></li>
            <li><a class="underline" href="interview/#system-design-interview">System design interview questions and exercises</a></li>
            <li><a class="underline" href="interview/#interview-readiness">14-point interview readiness checklist</a></li>
            <li><a class="underline" href="interview/#preparation-plan">Seven-session interview preparation plan</a></li>
            <li><a class="underline" href="interview/#interview-faq">Interview preparation FAQ</a></li>
        </ul>
    </nav>

    <section aria-labelledby="csharp-interview-questions" class="[ flex flex-col gap-4 ]">
        <h2 id="csharp-interview-questions" class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-2xl ] [ scroll-mt-20 ]">C# interview questions and answers</h2>
        <p>For each answer, write a small example and explain an edge case. Interviewers can then assess your reasoning, not just your recall.</p>
        @foreach (var question in CSharpQuestions)
        {
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h3 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">@question.Question</h3>
                <p>@question.Answer</p>
            </article>
        }
        <p>
            Practice with <a class="underline" href="blogs/oops-encapsulation/">encapsulation in C#</a>,
            <a class="underline" href="blogs/oops-abstraction/">abstraction in C#</a>,
            <a class="underline" href="blogs/understanding-linq-deferred-immediate-streaming-and-non-streaming-executions/">LINQ deferred and immediate execution examples</a>, and
            <a class="underline" href="blogs/design-pattern-introduction/">design patterns in C#</a>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="dotnet-interview-questions" class="[ flex flex-col gap-4 ]">
        <h2 id="dotnet-interview-questions" class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-2xl ] [ scroll-mt-20 ]">.NET and ASP.NET Core interview questions</h2>
        <p>
            These dotnet interview questions cover the platform, ASP.NET Core and MVC, Web API, and Entity Framework Core.
            For experienced roles, extend each answer with a failure you diagnosed or a trade-off you measured.
        </p>
        @foreach (var question in DotNetQuestions)
        {
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h3 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">@question.Question</h3>
                <p>@question.Answer</p>
            </article>
        }
        <p>
            Explore <a class="underline" href="channels/web-api/">ASP.NET Core Web API tutorials</a>,
            <a class="underline" href="blogs/webapi-importance-of-status-code/">Web API status codes</a>, and
            <a class="underline" href="channels/efcore/">EF Core tutorials</a> to turn short answers into working examples.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="system-design-interview" class="[ flex flex-col gap-4 ]">
        <h2 id="system-design-interview" class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-2xl ] [ scroll-mt-20 ]">System design interview questions for .NET developers</h2>
        <p>
            A system design interview evaluates how you turn uncertain requirements into an architecture and explain its trade-offs.
            Start with users, workloads, and reliability goals before choosing services. The process applies beyond .NET;
            ASP.NET Core is one possible implementation, not the design itself.
        </p>
        <h3 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">How to approach a system design interview</h3>
        <ol class="[ list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 ]">
            <li><strong>Clarify requirements:</strong> define the core user journeys, scope, latency target, availability goal, and data-retention constraints.</li>
            <li><strong>Estimate scale:</strong> state assumptions for requests per second, peak traffic, payload size, and storage growth. Use the estimates to justify decisions.</li>
            <li><strong>Define contracts and data:</strong> sketch API endpoints, entities, access patterns, indexes, and consistency requirements.</li>
            <li><strong>Draw the simplest viable design:</strong> begin with an API and a database; add caches, queues, replicas, or service boundaries only to address a stated need.</li>
            <li><strong>Test failure scenarios:</strong> discuss timeouts, retries with backoff, idempotency, backpressure, security, and recovery.</li>
            <li><strong>Explain trade-offs:</strong> identify bottlenecks, instrumentation, cost, and what you would change as traffic grows.</li>
        </ol>
        @foreach (var question in SystemDesignQuestions)
        {
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h3 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">@question.Question</h3>
                <p>@question.Answer</p>
            </article>
        }
        <h3 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">Practice exercises: design a system and defend your decisions</h3>
        <ul class="[ list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 ]">
            <li><strong>URL shortener:</strong> define create and redirect APIs, unique short-code generation, expiry, abuse prevention, and read-heavy caching. Explain collision handling and the effect of stale cached redirects.</li>
            <li><strong>Order processing:</strong> model order state, inventory reservations, payment idempotency, and asynchronous notifications. Explain how an outbox and idempotent consumers handle failures without claiming exactly-once delivery across services.</li>
            <li><strong>Notification service:</strong> separate request acceptance from delivery with a queue and workers. Define deduplication keys, retry limits, a dead-letter queue, provider rate limits, and delivery-status tracking.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            Build depth with <a class="underline" href="blogs/understanding-caching-fundamentals-in-dotnet/">caching fundamentals in .NET</a>,
            <a class="underline" href="blogs/avoiding-cache-stampede-and-handling-nulls-in-dotnet/">cache stampede prevention</a>,
            <a class="underline" href="channels/ddd/">domain-driven design</a>, and
            <a class="underline" href="channels/solid/">SOLID principles</a>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <h2 id="interview-readiness" class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-2xl ] [ scroll-mt-20 ]">Interview Readiness</h2>
    <p>
        Prepare to discuss how you deliver, design, test, and support software. These pointers are a checklist of areas to be aware of,
        not a script to memorize. Focus on examples from your own work and explain why your team made its choices.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="delivery-readiness-title" class="[ flex flex-col gap-4 ]">
        <h3 id="delivery-readiness-title" class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">Delivery and operations</h3>
        <div class="[ grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 ]">
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">1. Path to production</h4>
                <p>Describe how work moves through development, developer review, QA, pre-production or stakeholder approval, and production. Explain what each stage verifies.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">2. Deployment and infrastructure</h4>
                <p>Know how infrastructure is provisioned and releases are deployed. Examples include Terraform, AWS CloudFormation, Azure Bicep or ARM, and Kubernetes. Describe your team's actual approach.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">3. CI/CD pipelines</h4>
                <p>Be ready to walk through checkout, dependency installation and restore, build, security scanning, tests, coverage checks, and artifact or image publishing, followed by release gates. Pipeline examples include GitHub Actions, Azure DevOps, Jenkins, and Bamboo. For on-premises hosting, know options such as IIS on Windows and Apache or Nginx on Linux.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">4. Observability and telemetry</h4>
                <p>Explain how logs, metrics, and anomalies are monitored and how alerts reach the team. Tools may include Application Insights, CloudWatch, Datadog, or Splunk.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">5. Testing strategy</h4>
                <p>Understand the purpose of unit, integration (including Testcontainers), architecture fitness, mutation, and end-to-end tests. Distinguish pipeline automation from QA activities such as smoke, regression, and exploratory testing.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">6. Cloud resources</h4>
                <p>
                    Identify the compute, storage, database, messaging, networking, monitoring, and secret-management services your application uses.
                    AWS examples include Lambda, ECS or EKS, S3, DynamoDB, RDS, Route 53, SNS, SQS, CloudWatch, and Secrets Manager. Azure examples include
                    Functions, Container Apps or AKS, Blob Storage, Cosmos DB, Azure SQL, Azure DNS, Service Bus, Storage Queues, Application Insights,
                    and Key Vault. Explain what the services in your own system do.
                </p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="architecture-readiness-title" class="[ flex flex-col gap-4 ]">
        <h3 id="architecture-readiness-title" class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">Architecture and engineering</h3>
        <div class="[ grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 md:grid-cols-2 ]">
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">7. Backend architecture</h4>
                <p>Be prepared to describe a monolith, modular monolith, or microservices, along with module boundaries and communication. Know patterns such as Clean Architecture, Vertical Slice Architecture, contracts, event-driven design, and event storming, and explain why the chosen approach fits.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">8. Engineering practices</h4>
                <p>Have examples of TDD, DDD, SOLID, and design patterns. Also discuss cross-cutting concerns such as security, validation, error handling, resilience, caching, auditing, logging, and performance monitoring.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">9. Frontend architecture</h4>
                <p>Know the UI framework, such as Angular, React, Vue, Blazor, ASP.NET MVC, or Razor Pages, and whether the app is a monolith or uses micro-frontends. Be familiar with lazy loading, state management, identity-provider integration, and analytics where relevant.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">10. Technology versions</h4>
                <p>Know the major runtime and framework versions used in your project, plus how upgrades are managed. Check current support status; version examples become outdated.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">11. Database migrations</h4>
                <p>Explain how schema changes are authored, reviewed, and applied, whether through scripts, application migrations, or a migration bundle, and how the change fits the release and recovery plan.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">12. Feature toggles</h4>
                <p>Understand how feature or release flags enable controlled rollouts and A/B testing, who owns them, and how stale toggles are removed.</p>
            </article>
            <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
                <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">13. CAP theorem</h4>
                <p>Be able to explain the consistency and availability trade-off a distributed system faces when a network partition occurs, and relate it to the needs of a real system.</p>
            </article>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="experience-readiness-title" class="[ flex flex-col gap-4 ]">
        <h3 id="experience-readiness-title" class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">Experience and contribution</h3>
        <article class="[ rounded-xl border border-stone-200 bg-white p-5 ] [ dark:border-stone-700 dark:bg-stone-800 ]">
            <h4 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold ]">14. Contributions beyond project work</h4>
            <p>
                Prepare specific examples of contributions such as open-source work, mentoring, helping with interviews, or building a technical community.
                You can also discuss how you show technology leadership within your organization: delivering internal tech talks, contributing to engineering
                guilds, sharing standards or practices, and helping teams adopt useful improvements. Explain your role, the impact, and what you learned.
            </p>
        </article>
    </section>

    <p>
        You do not need to have used every tool or architecture listed here. Show how you think, make trade-offs, learn, and contribute;
        use real examples and be clear about what you personally did.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="preparation-plan" class="[ flex flex-col gap-4 ]">
        <h2 id="preparation-plan" class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-2xl ] [ scroll-mt-20 ]">Seven-session .NET interview preparation plan</h2>
        <p>Use one session per day or spread the work over several weeks. Move on when you can explain and demonstrate the concept, not just recognize its name.</p>
        <ol class="[ list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 ]">
            <li><strong>C# and OOP:</strong> explain the four OOP concepts, polymorphism, abstract classes, interfaces, and static classes; write an example of each.</li>
            <li><strong>LINQ and design patterns:</strong> demonstrate when a LINQ query executes, and implement two design patterns you could discuss from your own code.</li>
            <li><strong>ASP.NET Core and MVC:</strong> build a small controller and API endpoint; explain routing, model binding, validation, and HTTP status codes.</li>
            <li><strong>Entity Framework and SQL:</strong> inspect the SQL generated by an EF Core query, justify an index, and fix an N+1 query.</li>
            <li><strong>System design:</strong> choose an exercise above; sketch the APIs, data model, capacity assumptions, and failure handling.</li>
            <li><strong>Production experience:</strong> work through the 14-point checklist and prepare examples of a release, an incident, and a technical decision.</li>
            <li><strong>Mock interview:</strong> explain your design aloud, accept a changed requirement, and identify the answers that need more practice.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>
            Continue with the <a class="underline" href="learningpath/">.NET tutorial learning paths</a> or
            <a class="underline" href="channels/testing/">testing tutorials</a>.
            For broader job-search preparation, visit the <a class="underline" href="career/">developer career guide</a>.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="interview-faq" class="[ flex flex-col gap-4 ]">
        <h2 id="interview-faq" class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-2xl ] [ scroll-mt-20 ]">.NET interview preparation FAQ</h2>
        <h3 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">What should I study for a .NET developer interview?</h3>
        <p>Study C# and OOP concepts, LINQ, design patterns, ASP.NET Core and MVC, Web API, SQL, and Entity Framework Core. Then practice explaining how your application is deployed and monitored. Use the role description to decide which areas need the most depth.</p>
        <h3 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">What is the difference between basic and advanced C# interview questions?</h3>
        <p>Basic questions check definitions, such as OOP concepts, abstract classes, interfaces, and static classes. Advanced questions ask you to apply them: choose between IEnumerable and IQueryable for a query, justify a design pattern, or explain why a polymorphic call runs a particular implementation. Prepare a short code example for each answer.</p>
        <h3 class="[ dark:text-white ] [ font-semibold text-lg ]">How do .NET interview questions for experienced developers differ?</h3>
        <p>Experienced developers, including those with 5 or 10 years of experience, should be ready to go beyond definitions: diagnose a slow query, explain a production incident, compare architecture options, and defend a release or recovery strategy. Describe the constraints, your personal contribution, the outcome, and what you would improve. Expectations vary by employer and role.</p>
    </section>

</section>

@code {
    private const string Title = "C# & .NET Interview Questions and Answers | I ❤️ DotNet";
    private const string Description = "Prepare for C# and .NET interviews with practical questions and answers, ASP.NET Core topics, system design exercises, and a 14-point readiness checklist.";
    private string BaseUrl => configuration.GetValue<string>("baseUrl")!;
    private string PageUrl => $"{BaseUrl}interview/";

    private static readonly (string Question, string Answer)[] CSharpQuestions =
    [
        ("What are the four OOP concepts in C#?",
            "Encapsulation protects state and invariants behind an API. Abstraction exposes the essential behavior while hiding implementation details. Inheritance derives a type from a base type to reuse and specialize behavior. Polymorphism lets code use a common contract while dispatching to different implementations. For an order, keep state transitions inside methods such as Confirm rather than exposing unrestricted setters; use an interface when payment providers need interchangeable behavior."),
        ("What is polymorphism in C#?",
            "Polymorphism lets one contract have many implementations. Compile-time polymorphism is method overloading: methods share a name but have different parameter lists, and the compiler chooses one. Run-time polymorphism uses a virtual or abstract member that a derived class overrides, or an interface member that each class implements; the object's actual type decides which implementation runs. The new modifier hides a base member instead of overriding it, so a call through a base-type reference still runs the base implementation."),
        ("What is the difference between an abstract class and an interface in C#?",
            "An abstract class cannot be instantiated; it can hold instance state, constructors, implemented members, and abstract members that derived classes must implement. An interface defines a contract that unrelated types can implement; modern C# also supports default interface implementations. A class can inherit from only one class but implement multiple interfaces. Use an abstract class for a family of types that share state and behavior, and an interface for a capability that different types provide."),
        ("What is a static class in C#?",
            "A static class cannot be instantiated or inherited and can contain only static members. Use it for stateless helpers and extension methods, such as a set of string utilities. Static members are shared across the application, so mutable static state needs thread-safety and is hard to isolate in tests. When behavior has dependencies or needs substitution in tests, prefer an injected service over a static class."),
        ("What is the difference between IEnumerable<T> and IQueryable<T> in LINQ?",
            "IEnumerable<T> exposes enumeration; LINQ-to-Objects operators execute .NET delegates as values are enumerated. IQueryable<T> exposes an expression tree that a provider, such as EF Core, can translate into a query. Many operators defer work until enumeration. ToList materializes results; repeated enumeration can repeat work or database queries. Filter and project before materializing when the provider can translate the operation."),
        ("Which design patterns should a C# developer know for an interview?",
            "Know a few patterns well enough to explain the problem each solves and its trade-offs. Common examples are Singleton, Factory, and Builder for object creation; Adapter, Decorator, and Facade for structure; Strategy, Observer, and State for behavior; and Repository and Unit of Work for data access. Use a pattern from your own code: describe the problem, why the pattern fitted it, and what it cost. Avoid applying patterns where a simpler design would work.")
    ];

    private static readonly (string Question, string Answer)[] DotNetQuestions =
    [
        ("What is .NET, and how is it different from C#?",
            ".NET is a development platform with a runtime, libraries, and tooling. C# is one of the languages used to build applications on it. The runtime provides services such as garbage collection and exception handling; ASP.NET Core provides web application and API frameworks. You write C# code, the compiler produces intermediate language, and the .NET runtime executes it."),
        ("What is the difference between .NET Framework, .NET Core, and modern .NET?",
            ".NET Framework is the original Windows-only implementation and is maintained for existing applications. .NET Core was the cross-platform, open-source rewrite. From .NET 5 onward, the product dropped \"Core\" from its name and is simply .NET; it is the recommended choice for new applications. Follow the job description: an employer maintaining legacy Windows applications may use .NET Framework, so know which runtime they use and do not assume the APIs and hosting models are identical."),
        ("What is ASP.NET Core, and how is it different from ASP.NET?",
            "ASP.NET is the web framework for .NET Framework, which runs on Windows and is typically hosted in IIS. ASP.NET Core is its cross-platform successor for modern .NET: it can be hosted in Kestrel behind a reverse proxy or in IIS, has built-in dependency injection and configuration, and handles requests through a middleware pipeline. Application models such as MVC, Razor Pages, minimal APIs, and Blazor are built on ASP.NET Core."),
        ("How does the MVC pattern work in ASP.NET Core?",
            "Model-View-Controller separates responsibilities. Routing maps a request to a controller action. The action uses model binding and validation to read input, calls services that contain the business logic, and returns a result: a view for HTML pages, or data for an API. The view renders the model with Razor. Keep controllers thin, check ModelState or rely on [ApiController] validation, and keep business rules out of views."),
        ("How should a Web API report validation, authentication, and authorization failures?",
            "Use HTTP semantics consistently: 400 commonly represents invalid input, 401 means the request lacks valid authentication, and 403 means access is refused, typically because an authenticated caller lacks permission. Use 404 when a resource is not found and 409 for a conflict with current resource state. Return a consistent Problem Details response where appropriate, without leaking stack traces or secrets. Validate input and enforce authorization on the server."),
        ("What is Entity Framework Core, and how do you investigate a slow query?",
            "EF Core is an object-relational mapper: it maps classes to tables and translates LINQ queries into SQL. When a query is slow, measure it and inspect the generated SQL and the database execution plan. Look for missing or unsuitable indexes, excess rows or columns, N+1 queries, and unnecessary round trips. Project only needed fields, paginate large results, and use no-tracking queries for suitable read-only work. Verify each change against representative data.")
